      {  
         "id": 11,
         "text": "EPS systems must include fault detection mechanisms that alert the driver to steering torque malfunctions and, where possible, provide a fail-operational mode.",
         "analysis": {
            "compliance": true,
            "explanation": "This requirement ensures safety by mandating that EPS (Electric Power Steering) systems have built-in fault detection. This feature alerts drivers to any issues with the steering torque, which is crucial for maintaining control of the vehicle. Additionally, providing a fail-operational mode enhances reliability and safety by allowing the system to continue functioning in a limited capacity even if a malfunction occurs."
         }
      },
      {  
         "id": 49,
         "text": "Steering torque systems should have the ability to self-calibrate to compensate for sensor drift or degradation over time.",
         "analysis": {
            "compliance": true,
            "explanation": "This requirement addresses long-term reliability and accuracy of steering torque systems. By enabling self-calibration, the system can adjust for any changes in sensor performance due to drift or wear, ensuring consistent steering response and safety throughout the vehicle's lifespan."
         }
      }
   ]
}

