{  
   "requirements_analysis":{  
      "requirement_73":{  
         "description":"The ESC (Electronic Stability Control) system is required to use wheel speed sensors for the purpose of executing vehicle dynamics corrections accurately and reliably.",
         "implications":[  
            {
               "system_design":"Wheel speed sensors must be integrated into the ESC system design, ensuring they are positioned correctly on each wheel to provide accurate data."
            },
            {
               "data_processing":"The ESC system's software must include algorithms capable of interpreting wheel speed sensor data in real-time to make appropriate corrections for vehicle stability."
            },
            {
               "reliability_testing":"Extensive testing under various driving conditions is necessary to validate the reliability and accuracy of the ESC system's response based on wheel speed sensing."
            }
         ],
         "considerations":[  
            "Sensor calibration must be precise to ensure accurate readings.",
            "The system should have redundancy or fail-safes in case a sensor fails.",
            "Compatibility with different vehicle models and tire types may affect sensor performance."
         ]
      },
      "requirement_60":{  
         "description":"The control system linked to acceleration sensing must be designed to handle faults without causing sudden or unexpected throttle responses, ensuring driver safety and comfort.",
         "implications":[  
            {
               "fault_tolerance_design":"The system architecture should include fault detection mechanisms that can identify sensor malfunctions or data anomalies."
            },
            {
               "response_strategy":"Upon detecting a fault, the control system must have predefined strategies to maintain normal operation without abrupt changes in throttle behavior."
            },
            {
               "driver_notification_system":"In case of faults, the system may need to alert the driver through visual or auditory signals, depending on the severity and impact of the fault."
            }
         ],
         "considerations":[  
            "The system must differentiate between actual acceleration events and sensor errors.",
            "Redundancy in sensors or alternative data sources could be considered for critical applications.",
            "Regular maintenance and calibration schedules should be established to minimize the risk of faults."
         ]
      }
   },
   "conclusion":"Both requirements emphasize the importance of reliability, safety, and user experience in vehicle control systems. Requirement 73 focuses on the technical integration and performance of wheel speed sensors within the ESC system for stability corrections. In contrast, requirement 60 highlights the need for robust fault tolerance in acceleration sensing to prevent unexpected throttle behavior. Together, they underscore a comprehensive approach to designing advanced driver-assistance systems (ADAS) that enhance vehicle safety and drivability."
}

